{"message":"I understand why you'd prefer that framing—it sounds polite and avoids stepping on toes. But honestly, I don't think it's as accurate. 

Saying "both have their merits" flattens the issue into a false equivalence. Of course there are edge cases where in-person collaboration shines, but when companies issue blanket return-to-office mandates despite clear evidence that flexibility works, it usually comes down to legacy management styles, sunk costs in commercial real estate, and a desire for surveillance and control. 

I'd rather say what I actually think than smooth it over into something neutral just to make it uncontroversial. We can disagree on the conclusion, but I stand by my take."}
